Herpes Simplex (Cold Sores)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Grouped vesicles on erythematous base, Mucocutaneous junction location, Prodromal tingling/burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-023
Reason for Visit: Severe, diffuse oral pain and ulceration leading to acute inability to swallow fluids and impending dehydration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult experiencing a severe, painful vesicular eruption across the lips and entire oral cavity, resulting in odynophagia and a complete cessation of fluid intake for 18 hours. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for primary herpetic gingivostomatitis, assess hydration status, and provide immediate systemic antivirals and topical anesthetics to prevent imminent hospitalization for intravenous rehydration.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My whole mouth is covered in ulcers and it burns so much I can't even swallow a sip of water. I feel incredibly weak."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-day history of systemic malaise and a mild fever, which was suddenly followed 48 hours ago by intense prodromal tingling and burning across the lips. This rapidly evolved into a widespread eruption of grouped vesicles situated on an erythematous base at the mucocutaneous junction of the lips and extending into the oral cavity. Most vesicles have ruptured, leaving highly painful, shallow ulcerations with early haemorrhagic crusting on the lips. The extreme pain has caused severe odynophagia, preventing the patient from eating or drinking since yesterday evening. This appears to be a severe primary infection.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently lethargic and bed-bound due to pain and dehydration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute kidney injury and systemic compromise due to poor oral intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 108/68 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Ill-appearing, tachycardic, drooling slightly into a tissue to avoid swallowing, dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: The upper and lower lips demonstrate confluent, grouped vesicles and extensive shallow, punched-out ulcerations with haemorrhagic crusting. Examination of the oral cavity reveals intense, generalized gingival erythema and swelling, with multiple 2-3mm aphthous-like ulcers on the buccal mucosa and tongue. The palate is clear. Exquisite tenderness is noted upon any mucosal manipulation. Tender, enlarged regional submandibular lymphadenopathy is prominent bilaterally. Capillary refill is 3 seconds, indicating mild clinical dehydration.
Environmental Observations: Untouched water bottles near the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications. No history of immunosuppression.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Primary Herpetic Gingivostomatitis - Status: Acute, high acuity
Differentials:

Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease (Coxsackievirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pronounced gingival involvement (classic for primary HSV), severe haemorrhagic crusting of the lips, and absence of characteristic lesions on the palms and soles.

Stevens-Johnson Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of skin target lesions, lack of a clear drug trigger, and the localized nature of the mucocutaneous involvement without ocular or genital lesions.

Aphthous Stomatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of classic grouped vesicles on the external lips, high fever, and severe generalized gingival inflammation, which are not features of simple aphthous ulcers.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to dehydration risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic, severe case of primary herpetic gingivostomatitis causing critical odynophagia and impending severe dehydration. Triage correctly identified the cessation of oral intake and lethargy as highly concerning for systemic decompensation. Urgent after-hours clinical evaluation was vital to physically assess the airway, rule out an obstructing retropharyngeal process, and administer immediate topical anesthesia to facilitate a supervised fluid trial. Prescribing systemic antivirals and a viscous anesthetic mouthwash directly avoided an immediate emergency department admission for IV hydration by rapidly restoring the patient's ability to tolerate oral fluids safely at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered 10ml of viscous Lidocaine 2% oral solution for immediate mucosal anesthesia. Supervised a trial of oral fluid intake (patient successfully drank 200ml of cold water). Prescribed oral Valacyclovir 1000mg BD for 7 days. Prescribed regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen liquid for systemic analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: By swishing the numbing mouthwash exactly 10 minutes before every meal, you will temporarily freeze the painful nerve endings, allowing you to drink the fluids your body desperately needs to recover. By taking small, continuous sips of ice water rather than trying to drink a whole glass at once, you will gently maintain your hydration without overwhelming your sore throat.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 24 hours to confirm sustained fluid intake and monitor urine output.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you become completely unable to swallow fluids again, if you stop urinating for more than 8 hours, or if you become confused or dizzy when standing up, your spouse must take you to the Emergency Department immediately for IV fluids.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Primary herpes infections are severe and take significant time to heal. Hydration is the absolute priority over the next few days.
Clinical Course: The intense pain and fever should begin to improve within 48 to 72 hours on the antiviral medication. The ulcers will take 10 to 14 days to completely heal.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your urine output carefully; it must remain light yellow and frequent. Seek immediate emergency care if you become dizzy or stop passing urine.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is your body's first time encountering the cold sore virus, causing a massive, painful immune response inside your mouth. The antiviral pills will stop the virus from multiplying, but the numbing liquid is crucial to let you drink and prevent dehydration.
Actionable Steps: Avoid hot, spicy, or acidic foods. Focus purely on cold liquids and soft foods like ice cream or yogurt until the ulcers begin to close.

